  "vulnerability_information": "`UpdateCacheMiddleware` / `CacheMiddleware` generates the same page-cache key when a request header nominated by `Vary` is absent and when it is present with an empty field value. This can cause a response selected for one request to be returned for the other.\n\nRFC 9111 §4.1 says that an absent nominated request field can match only another request in which that field is also absent. HTTP field-value grammar permits an empty field value.\n\n\n\nsteps to reproduce\n\nUse the included local-only PoC:\n\n```sh\nPYTHONPATH=/path/to/django-6.1.1 python poc_django_vary_missing_empty.py\n```\n\nIt sets up a view where presence of `X-Scope` selects a `PRIVATE` response and the response declares `Vary: X-Scope`.\n\n1. `GET /account` with `X-Scope:` stores `PRIVATE`.\n2. `GET /account` with no `X-Scope` field returns `PRIVATE` from cache.\n3. The view invocation counter is `1`, proving the second response was a cache hit.\n\nObserved output:\n\n```text\nfirst=PRIVATE second=PRIVATE view_calls=1\nVULNERABLE: missing and empty varied headers were treated as one cache key\n```\n\nI also verified Django’s ASGI request adapter preserves the present-but-empty field as `META[\"HTTP_X_SCOPE\"] == \"\"`; the collapse happens in the cache-key generator.\n\n## Impact\n\nAn application that correctly uses `Vary` to separate representations based on a header’s presence can have a representation cached for a request with an empty header served to a request without that header, or vice versa. If either representation contains data intended only for the other request state, this is private-response disclosure through Django’s origin cache.\n\nThe attacker capability is only to send an empty request header and to cause/request cache population; no secret-key knowledge, raw SQL, or special deployment component is needed.",
